# Documentation sync policy

The procedure lives in the `cti-doc-sync` skill. These are the standing
judgments behind it, which the procedure alone does not convey.

## Make as few edits as possible

The measure of a good run is not how much changed. A run that correctly
concludes nothing material happened and changes nothing is a successful run.

## Material versus routine

Routine, and not a documentation change on its own:

- One more weekly report in an established series
- Another bulletin in an established category
- A hunt package following the established identifier scheme
- A file touched, moved or renamed with no change in what it is

Material, and worth a documentation change:

- A new artifact type or deliverable class
- A product removed or deprecated
- A changed cadence
- A new platform, integration or dependency, for example a change in which
  platform hunts are authored against
- A new automated pipeline or new tooling under `_tools` or `_skill_patches`
- A change in terminology or product positioning, for example the bulletin
  category taxonomy or the hunt identifier scheme
- A documentation statement that has become false

## Revision entries are earned

Append a revision row only when something substantive changed. A branding pass
on its own is not program history. Never edit a document solely so that a
revision entry can be written.

## Preservation

Preserve existing structure, formatting, tone and level of detail unless a
structural change is genuinely needed. Do not remove historical or contextual
information just because newer material exists. Do not rewrite unchanged
material.

## Word heading styles carry numbering

When inserting content into an existing document, clone an existing paragraph of
the target style and replace its text. Do not create a paragraph from the style
name: Word's heading styles in these documents carry list numbering, and a
paragraph created that way picks up a stray automatic number.

## The deck evolves, it does not accumulate

`CTI_AI_Strategy_Evolving.pptx` changes only when evidence supports a change in
strategy. Keep the GeneLabs layouts, master, palette and slide design intact and
reuse existing layouts rather than inventing new visual treatments. Every
strategic change traces to new evidence, a changed assumption or a development in
the portfolio, and that evidence is named in the run summary.

## Unattended runs

When the scheduler starts the run, nobody is watching. Do not ask questions.
Where a judgment call is needed, make the conservative choice, apply it, and flag
it under items requiring human review in the run summary.
